            <ArticleTitle>Safety and efficacy of beta agonists.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>618-22; discussion 623-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Both short-acting and long-acting beta agonists have been used for many years for the treatment of asthma. Short-acting beta agonists are life-saving and their role as rescue agents is unquestioned, but regular use is not recommended because of safety concerns and the effectiveness of asthma-controller medications. Long-acting beta agonists are effective controller medications but have safety issues, so their use should be restricted to patients who are not optimally controlled on first-line controllers such as inhaled corticosteroids. The effect of the beta receptor genotype on beta agonist response is unclear but could hold promise for proper patient management.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
